WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.630 --> 00:00:05.860
Since 2011, the stars of Impractical Jokers
have produced countless hours of self-humiliating

00:00:05.860 --> 00:00:09.950
comedy, and a pretty large number of running
gags along the way. Loyal viewers probably

00:00:09.950 --> 00:00:14.380
caught most of these weird references in Impractical
Jokers : The Movie, but are a few in case

00:00:14.380 --> 00:00:15.020
you missed them.

00:00:15.840 --> 00:00:20.300
Viewers of Impractical Jokers know that the
show focuses almost entirely on its four charming

00:00:20.310 --> 00:00:24.880
stars who have known each other since childhood
: Sal Vulcano, Joe Gatto, James Murray and

00:00:24.880 --> 00:00:29.509
Brian Quinn. But fans truly in the know understand
that they have a fifth member, and his name

00:00:29.509 --> 00:00:30.060
is Larry.

00:00:30.500 --> 00:00:32.760
"Joe, these FedExes need to go out. You need
Larry!”

00:00:33.200 --> 00:00:34.200
“LARRY!"

00:00:36.080 --> 00:00:40.500
Often screamed at, but never seen, Larry is
the go-to name for a guy who always seems

00:00:40.500 --> 00:00:45.030
to be missing. He's to blame for the circumstances
surrounding a number of challenges, and even

00:00:45.030 --> 00:00:49.120
though nobody knows what he actually looks
like, or where to find him, his presence is

00:00:49.120 --> 00:00:52.340
known. Larry makes his latest non-appearance
twice in The Movie.

00:00:52.600 --> 00:00:55.740
"Larry is the number one thing I hear screamed
at me when I walk down the street.”

00:00:55.740 --> 00:00:57.600
“I think the like Larry better than any
of us.”

00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:03.300
Larry's first non-cameo is a reference found
on Q's license plates, which start out "LRY".

00:01:03.680 --> 00:01:07.380
The second time Larry is mentioned is after
the group fakes their way past security at

00:01:07.399 --> 00:01:11.409
a Paula Abdul concert. Just as they sneak
through the doors, Q announces on a walkie

00:01:11.409 --> 00:01:16.990
talkie that they've taken the arena. The response
from the other end? "Where's Larry?" Not coincidentally,

00:01:16.990 --> 00:01:19.340
that was also the name of their 2015 tour.

00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:23.580
"LARRY! LARRY!"

00:01:24.560 --> 00:01:28.960
The entire imaginary setup of Impractical
Jokers : The Movie takes place on Staten Island

00:01:28.960 --> 00:01:33.590
in 1992, but look closely and you'll see that
Staten Island isn't all it seems.

00:01:34.280 --> 00:01:36.940
"We can go on a wacky adventure on Staten
Island.

00:01:37.100 --> 00:01:37.600
“no."

00:01:38.140 --> 00:01:42.220
Director Chris Henchy revealed in an interview
with S I Live that it was too expensive to

00:01:42.229 --> 00:01:46.270
film largely in Staten Island itself, and
production had to resort to finding streets

00:01:46.270 --> 00:01:50.469
in cheaper locations that were simply Staten
Island-ish. Fortunately, the scenes that were

00:01:50.469 --> 00:01:54.909
shot authentically feature some of the Jokers'
most iconic locations. You'll spot Monsignor

00:01:54.909 --> 00:01:58.450
Farrell High School, where the boys all went
to school together, and later returned for

00:01:58.450 --> 00:02:03.030
a lie detector punishment focused on Murr.
You'll also see Ladder Company 86, where Q

00:02:03.030 --> 00:02:06.420
once served as a firefighter before moving
on to become a TV big-shot.

00:02:07.280 --> 00:02:09.240
"I heard you were a great fireman. “

00:02:10.920 --> 00:02:13.460
“No. I’m an actor on television.”

00:02:14.260 --> 00:02:18.360
Ballbag. It's a word that basically means
nothing and everything, but when the boys

00:02:18.370 --> 00:02:22.380
are excitedly discussing the nature of having
exceptional testicular fortitude, the word

00:02:22.380 --> 00:02:23.900
'ballbag' is thrown around a lot.

00:02:24.310 --> 00:02:28.460
Sure, it's kinda funny on its surface, but
it's also a distinct callback to one specific

00:02:28.470 --> 00:02:33.170
Impractical Jokers episode, where it makes
its one and only memorable appearance. While

00:02:33.170 --> 00:02:36.890
acting as wall climbing instructors during
a challenge appropriately called "Repeat After

00:02:36.890 --> 00:02:40.510
Me', each one of the Jokers must get their
wall-climbing student to repeat a bizarre

00:02:40.510 --> 00:02:44.522
phrase or word given to them by the other
Jokers. While Murr tried and failed to have

00:02:44.522 --> 00:02:48.760
his student repeat "I'm going to die!", and
Q also failed to get his student to repeat

00:02:48.760 --> 00:02:53.380
"lumpenproletariat" , Joe succeeded in getting
his student to repeat 'ballbag' not once,

00:02:53.380 --> 00:02:54.380
but twice.

00:02:54.480 --> 00:02:55.480
"You wanna ballbag over there.”

00:02:55.600 --> 00:02:56.720
“Ballbag?”

00:02:57.820 --> 00:03:01.200
While you'd expect the Jokers' many recurring
characters to make appearances in their big

00:03:01.200 --> 00:03:06.050
screen debut, it was Murr's quiet and strange
Stanley Merkel who made the most notable appearance.

00:03:06.580 --> 00:03:09.160
"My name is Stanley Merkle and I’m checking
out.”

00:03:09.540 --> 00:03:13.510
The weird character originated as the winning
costume in a 'creep-off' competition filmed

00:03:13.510 --> 00:03:16.780
by the Jokers for their 2016 'Santiago Sent
Us' tour.

00:03:17.040 --> 00:03:21.320
Not happy to just leave Stanley in a video
clip only seen by tour attendees, Murr brought

00:03:21.320 --> 00:03:25.350
the character back for the TV show, during
a costume change competition in which the

00:03:25.350 --> 00:03:29.290
Jokers had to return to the same receptionist
multiple times without getting recognized,

00:03:29.290 --> 00:03:33.940
accomplished through the aid of ridiculous
costumes. Stanley returned once again in Impractical

00:03:33.940 --> 00:03:37.960
Jokers : The Movie as a random stranger who
shows up to sit on Santa's lap in the middle

00:03:37.960 --> 00:03:40.880
of a shopping mall. Santa, of course, is played
by Sal.

00:03:41.360 --> 00:03:44.180
"It was…. enlightening."

00:03:45.200 --> 00:03:48.920
The Jokers are known to wear shirts that promote
their own personal interests on the Impractical

00:03:48.930 --> 00:03:53.900
Jokers TV show. Joe often wears a shirt that
says 'Adopt Don't Shop', and Sal seems to

00:03:53.900 --> 00:03:55.520
never take off his Wu-Tang tee.

00:03:55.720 --> 00:03:59.910
And while Q can often be seen wearing shirts
that refer to his various pop culture loves,

00:03:59.910 --> 00:04:04.260
many of his shirts also refer to his podcast,
Tell 'Em Steve Dave, which he runs with Walt

00:04:04.260 --> 00:04:08.781
Flanagan and Bryan Johnson of Comic Book Men
fame. In Impractical Jokers : The Movie, it's

00:04:08.781 --> 00:04:13.420
an especially deep cut into Steve Dave lore
: a shirt that says 'Prussian Kissing Devil',

00:04:13.420 --> 00:04:18.290
which was discussed in depth in the 313th
episode of the podcast. Allegedly, the Prussian

00:04:18.290 --> 00:04:22.450
Kissing Devil was a strange sculptural head
that Q attempted to buy during his Thanksgiving

00:04:22.450 --> 00:04:26.880
break in rural Pennsylvania with his family.
Strangeness ensues, and the head found its

00:04:26.880 --> 00:04:29.900
way into TESD lore, and eventually onto a
t-shirt.

00:04:30.200 --> 00:04:34.140
And while we're on the subject of t-shirts,
Joe's legendary "I'm the Beef" shirt makes

00:04:34.140 --> 00:04:35.860
another cameo appearance as well.

00:04:36.060 --> 00:04:40.160
"I've been down here since 1987 I was just
looking for the gift shop!"

00:04:41.180 --> 00:04:45.250
The Jokers have attracted a pretty impressive
celebrity following, and their famous fanbase

00:04:45.250 --> 00:04:50.500
appears on their TV show often. Famous followers
include Rosie O'Donnell, Danica McKellar,

00:04:50.500 --> 00:04:53.380
and even Gary Busey has appeared in several
strange skits.

00:04:56.920 --> 00:05:01.120
"Don’t ever say that to me again the way
you said it to me yesterday."

00:05:01.640 --> 00:05:06.380
In Impractical Jokers : The Movie, however,
things get weird. While the boys play semi-fictionalized

00:05:06.380 --> 00:05:09.960
versions of themselves, their real-life friends
are employed as some of the characters that

00:05:09.960 --> 00:05:10.620
surround them.

00:05:11.040 --> 00:05:15.260
Easiest to spot is Casey Jost, a producer
of the show and host of Impractical Jokers

00:05:15.270 --> 00:05:19.960
: Inside Jokes, who appears here as Sam, a
close companion of Paula Abdul. He's easy

00:05:19.960 --> 00:05:24.790
to spot. Not quite as easy to spot is Kane
Hodder, the hulking actor who plays Paula

00:05:24.790 --> 00:05:28.720
Abdul's bodyguard later in the movie. He's
also one of the actors who's played Jason

00:05:28.720 --> 00:05:33.610
in the Friday the 13th series, and it's probably
no coincidence. It's well known that Q is

00:05:33.610 --> 00:05:37.530
a huge fan of horror, even wearing a Friday
the 13th hoodie during some challenges.

00:05:38.700 --> 00:05:42.540
When the Jokers parents show up, you know
that it's about to get really good.

00:05:42.780 --> 00:05:46.780
"Welcome to your father. Have a seat, your
ass is about to get punished."

00:05:47.220 --> 00:05:51.240
While most of the Jokers' parents have appeared
in various capacities, Joe Gatto's parents

00:05:51.240 --> 00:05:55.889
never showed up for one simple reason: they've
both passed away. Fortunately, it's a subject

00:05:55.889 --> 00:05:59.790
that Joe can still joke about. During one
waiting room challenge, where the Jokers all

00:05:59.790 --> 00:06:03.810
have to get each other to laugh in order to
achieve victory, Joe indicates that he's leaving

00:06:03.810 --> 00:06:07.820
to go retrieve his parents. He returns with
a pair of plastic skeletons.

00:06:08.060 --> 00:06:11.940
In what's perhaps the most touching hidden
detail in the entire 90 minutes, Joe brought

00:06:11.940 --> 00:06:17.060
his mother back one more time. In a June 2019
Instagram post, he revealed that he used his

00:06:17.060 --> 00:06:20.540
late mother's picture on an Employee of the
Month plaque hidden in the movie.

